#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087489 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7703063b-658d-50a5-8ac3-747b7f029bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at the Origin"}] :question/id #uuid "b798cfbb-89ac-5d31-adb5-5c43bd483226" :question/math-academy-id "e-5374" :question/problem "Consider the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown below. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(2,0).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/156d160267551e9dbf4db42b14b1e0dbb46ab658a836a760cf31a42eb89662d3.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$ centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius is $r = 2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087490 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0346056c-cf8e-51bf-92f3-052a0948a6fa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at a General Point"}] :question/id #uuid "140fc720-22c0-5832-afae-c255144291d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5375" :question/problem "Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ that generates a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(4,6).$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx= r\\cos\\theta+a,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta+b,\\quad  \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(4,6).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta+4,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta+6,\\quad\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087551 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d81c7ff-8459-5559-bfae-c2a9c3bc739e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "4ffaeeac-95a0-598a-9880-166d03f810ce" :question/math-academy-id "e-6779" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/f44fad12ad1f7d465423f92258caae38e0c536153ba2c3c3a6de573061c69993.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx = 6\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 6\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r = 6.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/4bd384e2b1a9d0568617ddbfd5ac7dbe9630882b45c40d7912f4809a6e0be551.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $\\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right],$ we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in \\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087555 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parametrized Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "8bff424d-8f2e-528b-acf0-12c33958a132" :question/math-academy-id "e-6817" :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta + 2,\\quad y=3\\sin\\theta - 4, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta\\lt 2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+4}{3} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x-2)^2 + (y+4)^2 = 9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7703063b-658d-50a5-8ac3-747b7f029bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9c61364-89e4-5ec3-a514-1fa886fddcb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=225\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=225\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285397} {:answer/id #uuid "3ca2e245-1032-5752-ade8-a7803af1ea17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{15}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{15}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285398} {:answer/id #uuid "bf6b8347-427c-5ff8-8fe1-dfdc0044329b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=30\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=30\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285399} {:answer/id #uuid "f73e5d31-eedb-523d-9bb1-d5f27f8eea2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=15\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285400} {:answer/id #uuid "e29315a3-c81d-5a6c-9918-c51edc8afbcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\sqrt{15}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f73e5d31-eedb-523d-9bb1-d5f27f8eea2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=15\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285400} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c35d5ed-8908-579f-bd21-599749407541" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "384a4738-eb41-5445-8951-5cd80374c2fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-105915" :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $15$ centered at the origin?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=15.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=15\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0346056c-cf8e-51bf-92f3-052a0948a6fa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bcb00fd-f159-5bf4-8043-906d1c3abcd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299479} {:answer/id #uuid "50881a7c-5ae8-555d-ba04-7ed120fe5f0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299480} {:answer/id #uuid "18f7dc68-6f40-56b8-b9d5-4c3f01828e79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299481} {:answer/id #uuid "2f9a090d-aaf9-5394-8e9c-0c7633220598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299482} {:answer/id #uuid "df72ae08-5e83-5454-8bd9-640c7c40ef33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f9a090d-aaf9-5394-8e9c-0c7633220598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299482} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac316a2d-1ff1-5d3c-aeb2-22bd7ef57252" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c45d1161-cc6e-56e5-89da-543c3e758d02" :question/math-academy-id "q-106051" :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(-3,2)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(-3,2).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299498 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7703063b-658d-50a5-8ac3-747b7f029bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d62cf37c-d928-5471-a730-29da52a950e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\cos(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d62cf37c-d928-5471-a730-29da52a950e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\cos(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299500} :answer-field/id #uuid "74e501c9-f1e9-510e-8202-362c8e8398e7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299499} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4412556e-6fb8-579f-ae78-4929da26e1aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\sin(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4412556e-6fb8-579f-ae78-4929da26e1aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\sin(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299502} :answer-field/id #uuid "54e65f65-c0b2-5b94-8cf9-7800c70d0e79" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d1d3adf2-769c-543a-8d79-ad858b0c058d" :question/math-academy-id "q-321891" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/da3a33a3ade10663064516bac22c07459ccf7ce556753ba94d70ee832e947458.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $10$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(10,0).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius is $r=10.$ Substituting $r=10$ gives\n\n$$\nx=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d81c7ff-8459-5559-bfae-c2a9c3bc739e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2a75d7f-a83a-53d2-91cf-19783575a28b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299545} {:answer/id #uuid "f25996bf-fd27-5e29-89e3-7d488619155e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299546} {:answer/id #uuid "22c3653e-9278-5059-86c9-30d610217067" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299547} {:answer/id #uuid "fc478a22-4762-53c5-90b4-bcbae6233313" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299548} {:answer/id #uuid "e655fb51-22af-560b-911f-fc4ad0e14612" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc478a22-4762-53c5-90b4-bcbae6233313" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299548} :answer-field/id #uuid "978b3bf0-a398-5c0c-a0df-b249d600a836" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9a0aa32-a01e-55d8-a12f-e34bc87e72ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-79166" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/5ac5a6ba5e7c041adfe2a36f9e7d835e8090a296cb72adffa5123f67cbed8aa3.png)\n\nThe three-quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=3.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a three-quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/6f27674dd7382f9040cdeb8e85ea35ff63e581ef5752b373aa0ab87a0b3e2f0f.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]$, we get the required three-quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the three-quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}]}
